    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the two new acceptance tests.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**`LocalityResolver.java`** \u2014 added a small, cohesive overload alongside the existing city-only method (which the `PostcodeValidator` still relies on, so its behaviour is untouched):\n\n```java\nprivate static final Map<Integer, String> POSTCODE_REGION =\n    Map.of(20, \"NSW\", 30, \"VIC\", 40, \"QLD\");\n\npublic static String locality(String city, String postcode) {\n    if (postcode != null) {\n        String region = POSTCODE_REGION.get(Integer.parseInt(postcode) / 100);\n        if (region != null) {\n            return region;\n        }\n    }\n    return locality(city);\n}\n```\n\nThe `/ 100` prefix maps each range exactly (2000\u20132099 \u2192 20 \u2192 NSW, etc.); an out-of-range or absent postcode falls through to the city table.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now passes the postcode too: `locality(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())`.\n\n## Structural-impact notes\n- **2 files touched**, both edits tiny.\n- I kept the original `locality(String)` method unchanged and added a **new** method rather than growing the existing one \u2014 the new method has low CC (2 branches) and `LocalityResolver`'s `WMC_other` is minimal.\n- The mapper change is a one-token edit to a single annotation expression, so its per-function cost stays low despite `OwnerMapper`'s larger surrounding complexity.\n- No new class, no duplicated range logic beyond a small prefix map local to the resolver.\n\nKnown cities still resolve to the same region, while cities that share a name are now disambiguated by their postcode.",
